                        "form": "die Kriegsmänner"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "596df18b-ca3e-3dc4-8c05-19f6a2cc6d8d",
                        "entry_id": "8ff446a6-1d56-32df-8638-64475b511c12",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Krieger"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "outdated"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "92f566ed-5c44-4adf-bb2e-204b135be234",
                                "sense_id": "596df18b-ca3e-3dc4-8c05-19f6a2cc6d8d",
                                "text": "„Das lateinische bonus glaube ich als ‚den Krieger‘ auslegen zu dürfen: vorausgesetzt, daß ich mit Recht bonus auf ein älteres duonus zurückführe […]. Bonus somit als Mann des Zwistes, der Entzweiung (duo), als Kriegsmann: man sieht, was im alten Rom an einem Manne seine ‚Güte‘ ausmachte.“"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5bb41880-e074-47d5-a6ed-293d8337fb70",
                                "sense_id": "596df18b-ca3e-3dc4-8c05-19f6a2cc6d8d",
                                "text": "„Ich fand mich für einen Kriegsmann wie geschaffen und kam mir bewundernswert vor.“"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "652ba48f-186e-4802-bdcf-b996594471ed",
                                "sense_id": "596df18b-ca3e-3dc4-8c05-19f6a2cc6d8d",
                                "text": "„Die Stuben der Bauern hatten kleine Glasfenster mit Gardinen, junge Damen guckten heraus und sahen die Kriegsmänner an, die aufgebrochen waren, um in diesem Matsch für den König die Schweden zu verdreschen; doch sie waren so durchnäßt und abgerackert, daß sie für die Mädchen keinen Gedanken übrighatten.“"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e7b41e05-fc25-4439-809a-92b84dda4e80",
                                "sense_id": "596df18b-ca3e-3dc4-8c05-19f6a2cc6d8d",
                                "text": "„Auch bei gewöhnlichen Kriegsmännern ist sie gewissermaßen das Zentrum des gesamten Auftretens, das manche gockelhaft nennen.“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "fdbf715a-77a3-4cb0-905c-0a56f043ae08",
                        "source_entry_id": "8ff446a6-1d56-32df-8638-64475b511c12",
                        "source_sense_id": "596df18b-ca3e-3dc4-8c05-19f6a2cc6d8d",
                        "source_sense_description": "veraltet: Krieger",
                        "target_lang_word": "warrior",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "warrior",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "63cca0dd-1a30-3318-884e-9a86be96f547",
                                        "entry_id": "08a41d82-fbf5-30d7-b99d-dccffd9e3700",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A person who is actively engaged in battle, conflict or warfare; a soldier or combatant."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ee1d032e-5c09-43e9-85a9-7ecbb6d6148c",
                                                "sense_id": "63cca0dd-1a30-3318-884e-9a86be96f547",
                                                "text": "Orion hit a rabbit once; but though sore wounded it got to the bury, and, struggling in, the arrow caught the side of the hole and was drawn out.[…]. Ikey the blacksmith had forged us a spearhead after a sketch from a picture of a Greek warrior; and a rake-handle served as a shaft."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7ba8db62-6ad4-45b6-9b1c-f8a010b926b9",
                                                "sense_id": "63cca0dd-1a30-3318-884e-9a86be96f547",
                                                "text": "[Link:] Gee, it sure is boring around here.\n [King Harkinian:] My boy, this peace is what all true warriors strive for."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "434aba44-e3e8-341b-99d0-8ffeb7a152d9",
                                        "entry_id": "08a41d82-fbf5-30d7-b99d-dccffd9e3700",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A person who is aggressively, courageously, or energetically involved in an activity, such as athletics."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "figuratively"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "6f14377a-b8bb-47e8-9177-0abdbb239d37",
                        "source_entry_id": "8ff446a6-1d56-32df-8638-64475b511c12",
                        "source_sense_id": "596df18b-ca3e-3dc4-8c05-19f6a2cc6d8d",
                        "source_sense_description": "veraltet: Krieger",
                        "target_lang_word": "man-of-war",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "man-of-war",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6197231c-ecce-3dea-b958-8ebbed9b8e67",
                                        "entry_id": "3209923a-49f4-3c7d-a979-45accaa16a1d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A man whose occupation is fighting in wars; a soldier, a warrior."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9722ce62-3733-4714-90cc-a29055da7495",
                                                "sense_id": "6197231c-ecce-3dea-b958-8ebbed9b8e67",
                                                "text": "The LORDE is the right man of warre, LORDE is his name. The charettes of Pharaor his power, hath he caſt in to the ſee."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1b14c4bb-15d2-41aa-b67c-65f11e8da4ad",
                                                "sense_id": "6197231c-ecce-3dea-b958-8ebbed9b8e67",
                                                "text": "In an hour more, the whole house was awakened by a violent noise of howling, curses, and pots clattering to and fro. […] [T]he landlord was undermost, and the Ensign's arms were working up and down his face and body like the flaps of a paddle-wheel: the man of war had clearly the best of it."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1d2a0628-7d3a-447c-8a38-d5231ce7f6e2",
                                                "sense_id": "6197231c-ecce-3dea-b958-8ebbed9b8e67",
                                                "text": "[T]he distinguished man of war, and worshipful scholar, Master Bloxham, was now promoted to take the tolls, and catch all the rebels around our part."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "13d4d96c-49de-391f-ae67-68975408501c",
                                        "entry_id": "3209923a-49f4-3c7d-a979-45accaa16a1d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A powerful armed naval vessel, primarily one armed with cannon and propelled by sails; a warship."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2835663b-480f-4264-ac2f-a2598883b071",
                                                "sense_id": "13d4d96c-49de-391f-ae67-68975408501c",
                                                "text": "[W]e cannot be ſecure, vvhile ſuch huge Fleets of Men of VVar, both Spaniſh, French, Dutch, and Dunkirkeers, ſome of them laden vvith Ammunition, Men, Arms, and Armies, do daily ſail on our Seas, and confront the Kings Chambers; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c6d52c11-2686-4fbc-b0aa-cbb716c15d14",
                                                "sense_id": "13d4d96c-49de-391f-ae67-68975408501c",
                                                "text": "In the lobby I spoke with Mr. George Montagu, and advised about a ship to carry my Lord Hinchingbroke and the rest of the young gentlemen to France, and they have resolved of going in a hired vessell from Rye, and not in a man of war."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b86e2689-2849-4f62-8305-44d1c437ffc3",
                                                "sense_id": "13d4d96c-49de-391f-ae67-68975408501c",
                                                "text": "[W]e ventured up to the mouth of the inner harbour, […] the entrance of the harbour blocked up by ſeveral old galleons and tvvo men of vvar that the enemy had ſunk in the channel."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "31b24840-5c3f-4e8b-9b9d-4d37fba6d6b2",
                                                "sense_id": "13d4d96c-49de-391f-ae67-68975408501c",
                                                "text": "[N]o wonder that, although junior partner, and as modest as he was high-spirited, he trod his counting-house floor with a step vigorous and springy as the young captain of a man-of-war, for he felt that he was an emancipated slave; nay, more, a British merchant."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "25fe292e-9932-4b3f-a6ed-ce7c6eb1fa7e",
                                                "sense_id": "13d4d96c-49de-391f-ae67-68975408501c",
                                                "text": "Well, I've not served the king for seven years for nothing, […] and I hope, sir, not heard the bullets whistling about my head like hail in a hail storm, without knowing how to take care of my ship. I like every thing man-of-war fashion, and then one's always prepared."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "80a77556-9096-4c2b-a38b-c776e5e06a1a",
                                                "sense_id": "13d4d96c-49de-391f-ae67-68975408501c",
                                                "text": "And there is kind of no ship or boat, built to swim in the sea, […] which does not lie at anchor in the Thames, somewhere between Greenwich Reach and London Bridge. […] [A]lso His Majesty's men-of-war—frigates, sloops-of-war, cutters, fire-ships, and every kind of vessel employed to beat off the enemies of the country, who would prey upon our commerce and destroy our merchantmen."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "212ceebf-192c-49f3-a64b-95a5d6ec70b9",
                                                "sense_id": "13d4d96c-49de-391f-ae67-68975408501c",
                                                "text": "His little man-o'-war top and unmentionables were full of sand but Cissy was a past mistress in the art of smoothing over life's tiny troubles and and^([sic]) very quickly not one speck of sand was to be seen on his smart little suit."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ab0f77c0-d8e3-3e46-987d-f67f2adb9add",
                                        "entry_id": "3209923a-49f4-3c7d-a979-45accaa16a1d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A powerful armed naval vessel, primarily one armed with cannon and propelled by sails; a warship.",
                                            "In full man-of-war's-man: a sailor serving on board an armed naval vessel."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical",
                                            "obsolete",
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "224d5f38-0314-46e8-a538-b1b0b8ac2079",
                                                "sense_id": "ab0f77c0-d8e3-3e46-987d-f67f2adb9add",
                                                "text": "[H]ee is firſt broken to the Sea in the Herring mans Skiffe or Cockboate, vvhere hauing learned to brooke all vvaters, and drinke as he can out of a tarrie Canne, and eate poore Iohn out of ſvvuttie platters vvhen he may get it vvithout butter or muſtard, there is no ho vvith him but once hartned thus, hee vvill needes be a man of vvarre, or a Tobaco taker, and vveare a ſiluer VVhiſtle."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "97e935d2-c281-3801-a5f8-4a3ee2b0a093",
                                        "entry_id": "3209923a-49f4-3c7d-a979-45accaa16a1d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Senses relating to animals.",
                                            "Ellipsis of man-of-war bird or (obsolete) man-of-war hawk (“any of a number of seabirds, especially one which attacks other seabirds to take their food”)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "abbreviation",
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "ellipsis"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9a9d2ba1-2f02-40b5-b0f6-ae67d051190a",
                                                "sense_id": "97e935d2-c281-3801-a5f8-4a3ee2b0a093",
                                                "text": "But there is a Bird they call, a Man of vvar, and he is much bigger than a Heron, and flies out to Sea upon diſcoveries, (for they never light upon the Sea) to ſee vvhat ſhips are comming to the Iland; and vvhen they return the Ilanders look out, and ſay, A ſhip is comming, and finde it true."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7496fbc9-ac16-3c06-a9cd-1ddfb2068a3e",
                                        "entry_id": "3209923a-49f4-3c7d-a979-45accaa16a1d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Senses relating to animals.",
                                            "Ellipsis of man-of-war bird or (obsolete) man-of-war hawk (“any of a number of seabirds, especially one which attacks other seabirds to take their food”).",
                                            "A frigatebird (family Fregatidae), especially the magnificent frigatebird (Fregata magnificens)."
                                        ],
